Awesome — glad these are helpful. I can generate the actual Google Calendar import file (CSV or ICS) for you — I just need the start date and your time zone (e.g., start on Monday 2026-05-11, America/Los_Angeles). If you share that, I’ll create a ready-to-import CSV you can drop into Google Calendar with all week events and recurring meetings.

In the meantime, here are the two items you asked for right away: a copy-paste GitHub PR template (drop in .github/PULL_REQUEST_TEMPLATE.md) and a sample GitHub Actions workflow you can add to pilot the PR SLA reminders.

1) GitHub PR template (copy-paste)
Save as .github/PULL_REQUEST_TEMPLATE.md

Title: [Short descriptive title]

Summary
- What: (short summary of the change)
- Why: (business reason / user problem solved)
- Linked ticket(s): #ISSUE_NUMBER

Acceptance criteria
- [ ] AC1 — expected behaviour
- [ ] AC2 — performance / edge cases

Change details
- Scope: (small / medium / large)
- Files / areas touched:
- Migration notes (if any):

Testing & verification
- How to test locally (steps)
- Test accounts / test data
- QA checklist:
  - [ ] Unit tests added/updated
  - [ ] Integration tests added/updated
  - [ ] Manual smoke test described & passed

Screenshots / recordings
- (Paste screenshots or link to recording)

Feature flags & rollout
- Feature flag name: feature.{name}
- Rollout plan (canary %, groups, timeline)

Backwards compatibility / data migrations
- (Any DB changes or user-facing migration steps)

Rollback plan
- Steps to rollback or disable if problems occur

Monitoring & dashboards to watch
- Dashboards: [link], key metrics: activation rate, D1/D7 retention, error rate, latency

Security / performance considerations
- (Any security review or perf testing required)

Notes / additional context
- (Links to design doc, RFC, discussion)

2) GitHub Actions workflow: PR review reminder (sample)
Drop in .github/workflows/pr-review-reminder.yml

This workflow runs on a schedule, finds open PRs older than N hours with no reviews, and posts a friendly reminder comment. Test in a test repo first to tune cadence (hourly may be noisy — consider daily).

Copy-paste YAML:

name: "PR Review Reminder"

on:
  schedule:
    # runs every 4 hours — adjust as needed. Use cron in UTC.
    - cron: '0 */4 * * *'
  workflow_dispatch:

jobs:
  remind:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - name: Remind on stale PRs lacking reviews
        uses: actions/github-script@v6
        with:
          script: |
            const HOURS_THRESHOLD = 24; // change to 24/48 etc.
            const MIN_PR_AGE_MS = HOURS_THRESHOLD * 60 * 60 * 1000;
            const now = new Date();

            // labels or title prefixes to ignore (WIP)
            const IGNORE_LABELS = ["WIP","do-not-review","draft"];
            const IGNORE_TITLE_KEYWORDS = ["WIP","WIP:", "DO NOT REVIEW"];

            const { data: prs } = await github.pulls.list({
              owner: context.repo.owner,
              repo: context.repo.repo,
              state: "open",
              per_page: 100
            });

            let reminders = 0;
            for (const pr of prs) {
              // skip drafts
              if (pr.draft) continue;

              // skip by label
              const prLabels = pr.labels.map(l => l.name.toLowerCase());
              if (prLabels.some(l => IGNORE_LABELS.map(x=>x.toLowerCase()).includes(l))) continue;

              // skip by title keywords
              if (IGNORE_TITLE_KEYWORDS.some(k => pr.title.toUpperCase().includes(k.toUpperCase()))) continue;

              const createdAt = new Date(pr.created_at);
              if ((now - createdAt) < MIN_PR_AGE_MS) continue;

              // fetch reviews for the PR
              const { data: reviews } = await github.pulls.listReviews({
                owner: context.repo.owner,
                repo: context.repo.repo,
                pull_number: pr.number,
                per_page: 100
              });

              // consider a PR "reviewed" if there is at least one APPROVED / CHANGES_REQUESTED / COMMENT review
              const hasReview = reviews && reviews.length > 0;

              // also check requested reviewers
              const hasRequestedReviewers = (pr.requested_reviewers && pr.requested_reviewers.length > 0);

              // only remind if no substantive reviews and no reviewer requested
              if (!hasReview && !hasRequestedReviewers) {
                // Post a comment reminder
                const commentBody = `Hi @${pr.user.login} — this PR has been open for more than ${HOURS_THRESHOLD} hours with no reviews requested. Please either:
- add a reviewer (request a review), or
- mark as WIP/draft if it’s not ready.

If you’d like, tag @engineering-lead to help assign reviewers.`;

                await github.issues.createComment({
                  owner: context.repo.owner,
                  repo: context.repo.repo,
                  issue_number: pr.number,
                  body: commentBody
                });
                reminders++;
              }
            } // end for
            core.info(`PR review reminder run complete. Comments posted: ${reminders}`);

Notes & next steps for the Action
- Adjust HOURS_THRESHOLD and cron cadence to what you want (24/48 hours, run daily at 09:00 UTC, etc.).
- This simple version posts a comment on PRs that have no reviews or requested reviewers. You can extend it to:
  - ping a reviewer rotation list (like @team/reviewers) or add a label "review-needed".
  - only target PRs with a specific size label (e.g., only "small" or "medium").
- Test in a sandbox repo first. The action uses the GITHUB_TOKEN and will post comments as the workflow actor.
- If you prefer an app: consider using third-party tools like Pull Reminders, ReviewFlow, or GitHub's built-in CODEOWNERS + required reviews to gate merges.

3) Quick rollout plan to pilot the SLA
- Add the PR template to .github/PULL_REQUEST_TEMPLATE.md.
- Add the workflow to .github/workflows/pr-review-reminder.yml in a branch and run by workflow_dispatch to test.
- In a kickoff meeting with engineering lead (15–30 min) agree:
  - Size thresholds (small/medium/large LOC or scope).
  - SLAs: small = 24h, medium = 48–72h, large = 5 business days.
  - Reviewer rotation owner and who will own the action automation.
- Run pilot for 4 weeks; track metrics: avg review time, % PRs meeting SLA, PR age distribution.

Next step: Calendar invites
If you want, reply with:
- Start date for Week 1 (e.g., Monday YYYY-MM-DD)
- Your time zone (IANA format like America/Los_Angeles)
- Preferred daily time window for one-off events (e.g., all-day week marker or 9:30–10:30 local time)
I’ll generate a CSV you can import to Google Calendar (or an ICS if you prefer) with:
- Week-by-week event entries (Weeks 1–12)
- Recurring events: weekly team sync + biweekly 1:1 template (I’ll include instructions for setting recurrence during import)
- Descriptions populated with the task lists and owners for each event

